---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc972.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Common Property — the Capitalization Policy Choice" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Regular vs Special Assessments — Revenue Recognition" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Replacement Fund (Reserve) Accounting" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Worked Example — the Common Property Capitalization Choice Illustrated" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Worked Example — a Special Assessment" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s10", label: "No Dedicated IFRS CIRA Standard — Discussion" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">An association that manages, but doesn't economically "own," common property for itself</div><div class="principle">A Common Interest Realty Association (CIRA) — a homeowners association, condominium association, or housing cooperative — exists to MANAGE common property on behalf of its MEMBER owners, who collectively hold the real economic interest. This raises a genuinely UNUSUAL question: should the association itself CAPITALISE common property (roads, clubhouses, shared infrastructure) as ITS OWN asset, given it doesn't hold that property for its OWN economic benefit in the way a typical entity would? ASC 972 addresses this through a distinctive, EXPLICIT accounting POLICY choice (Section 3).</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Homeowners associations, condominium associations, and housing cooperatives</div><div class="principle">ASC 972 applies to CIRAs — associations of property owners formed to manage and maintain COMMON property and enforce community rules, funded through member ASSESSMENTS.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Common Property — the Capitalization Policy Choice</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A genuinely unusual, explicit either/or accounting policy election</div><div class="principle">ASC 972 EXPLICITLY PERMITS a CIRA to EITHER capitalise common property (roads, recreational facilities, shared infrastructure) as its OWN asset (depreciated like normal PP&amp;E), <strong>OR</strong> to NOT capitalise it at all — reflecting the reasoning that, since the association typically CANNOT sell or otherwise economically benefit from the common property independently of its MEMBERS' collective ownership interest, capitalisation may not always provide meaningful information. This is a genuinely UNUSUAL feature — DIVERGENT industry practice exists, with SOME associations capitalising common property and others NOT, both considered ACCEPTABLE under ASC 972, PROVIDED the POLICY is applied CONSISTENTLY and disclosed.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Regular vs Special Assessments — Revenue Recognition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Ongoing operating dues vs one-time capital assessments</div><div class="principle">REGULAR assessments (ongoing periodic dues covering routine operating costs) are recognised as REVENUE over the period they relate to, similar to ordinary membership dues. SPECIAL assessments (one-time charges levied for a SPECIFIC capital purpose, e.g. a major roof replacement) are recognised as revenue based on the SPECIFIC purpose and TIMING of the underlying capital project, often DEFERRED until the related expenditure is actually incurred.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Replacement Fund (Reserve) Accounting</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Funding studies supporting major future repair/replacement needs</div><div class="principle">Many CIRAs maintain a REPLACEMENT (reserve) FUND — accumulated resources set aside for FUTURE major repairs and replacements of common property components (roofs, roads, pools) — often informed by a periodic "RESERVE STUDY" or "FUNDING STUDY" estimating future capital needs and the contribution levels required to meet them. Reserve fund contributions and related restricted cash/investments are typically presented SEPARATELY from operating fund resources, given their DIFFERENT intended purpose and often legally or contractually RESTRICTED nature.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example — the Common Property Capitalization Choice Illustrated</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Two acceptable outcomes for the same underlying facts</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A community clubhouse and pool</div><div class="wk-body">A homeowners association owns a $2,500,000 clubhouse and pool complex, used exclusively by association members.</div><div class="wk-answer"><strong>Under Policy A (capitalization):</strong> the association records the $2,500,000 clubhouse/pool as a PP&amp;E ASSET, depreciated over its useful life, appearing on the association's balance sheet.<br><strong>Under Policy B (non-capitalization):</strong> the SAME $2,500,000 clubhouse/pool is simply NOT recorded as an asset at all on the association's balance sheet, reflecting the view that the association doesn't hold it for its OWN independent economic benefit. <strong>BOTH approaches are ACCEPTABLE</strong> under ASC 972, provided the elected policy is applied CONSISTENTLY and clearly disclosed.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — a Special Assessment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Deferred until the related capital project occurs</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A special roof replacement assessment</div><div class="wk-body">A condominium association levies a $300,000 special assessment on its members to fund an upcoming roof replacement project expected to begin in 6 months.</div><div class="wk-answer">The $300,000 collected is initially recorded as DEFERRED revenue (or a restricted fund balance) — recognised as REVENUE (and the roof replacement expenditure capitalised, if the association's policy capitalises common property, or expensed if not) as the roof replacement PROJECT actually PROCEEDS, rather than recognised immediately upon collection.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 972 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>The elected common property capitalization policy, applied consistently</span><span>Nature and status of replacement fund (reserve) resources and any recent funding study results</span><span>Regular vs special assessment revenue, separately identified</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether to capitalise common property, and applying that elected policy consistently</span></div></div>
    <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Future replacement fund needs, informed by periodic reserve/funding studies</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>No Dedicated IFRS CIRA Standard — Discussion</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">General principles apply, without an explicit policy-choice mechanism</div><div class="principle">IFRS has NO dedicated CIRA industry standard, and — notably — does NOT provide the SAME explicit "either capitalise or don't" POLICY CHOICE that ASC 972 offers for common property. An IFRS-reporting association would need to apply GENERAL asset recognition principles (control, probable future economic benefit) to determine whether common property should be recognised — likely arriving at a MORE consistent, less policy-dependent answer than ASC 972's genuinely unusual explicit dual-option approach.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 972 event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Common property (if capitalized policy elected)</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Recognised as PP&amp;E, depreciated</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Regular assessments</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised as revenue over the period</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Special assessments</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Deferred until the related capital project proceeds</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 360</span><span>Supplies the general PP&amp;E depreciation framework applied if the capitalization policy is elected</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 970</span><span>Real Estate—General — supplies broader real estate cost capitalisation concepts</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Common property</div><div class="glossary-def">Shared infrastructure and facilities managed by a CIRA on behalf of its members, which may or may not be capitalized as an asset under an elected accounting policy.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Special assessment</div><div class="glossary-def">A one-time charge levied on members for a specific capital purpose, generally deferred until the related expenditure is incurred.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Replacement fund (reserve fund)</div><div class="glossary-def">Accumulated resources set aside for future major repairs and replacements of common property, informed by periodic funding studies.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc972 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
